          %h1#twovect_a_mathematica_package_for_2vector_spaces
            %em> TwoVect
            \: a Mathematica Package for 2-Vector Spaces
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#what_is_twovect"}
                  What is
                  = succeed "?" do
                    %em TwoVect
              %li
                %a{:href => "#how_can_i_get_it"} How can I get it?
              %li
                %a{:href => "#whats_it_for"} What’s it for?
              %li
                %a{:href => "#how_does_it_work"} How does it work?
              %li
                %a{:href => "#how_could_this_be_developed"} How could this be developed?
          %h2#what_is_twovect
            What is
            = succeed "?" do
              %em TwoVect
          %p
            %em TwoVect
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mathematica"} Mathematica
            package for working with finite-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex
            %a{:href => "semisimple+category"} semisimple
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-vector+spaces"} 2-vector spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %strong 2Vect
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+bicategory"} symmetric monoidal bicategory
            of finite-dimensional 2-vector spaces.
          %p
            2-vector space are categories with many of the same properties as ordinary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kapranov-Voevodsky+2-vector+spaces"} Kapranov-Voevodsky 2-vector spaces
            %a{:href => "2-Hilbert+space"} 2-Hilbert spaces
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "John+Baez"} Baez
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "quantum+group"} quantum algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theory"} topological quantum field theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information"} quantum information
          %p
            %em TwoVect
            was developed by
            %a{:href => "http://ctpweb.lns.mit.edu/personnel.html"} Dan Roberts
            in 2011 as an MSc project, at the
            %a{:href => "http://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/activities/quantum/"} Quantum Group
            in the
            %a{:href => "http://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/"} Department of Computer Science
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/"} University of Oxford
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/jamie.vicary"} Jamie Vicary
          %h2#how_can_i_get_it How can I get it?
          %p
            Here are download links for the
            %a{:href => "http://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/jamie.vicary/twovect/twovect.zip"} packages
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/jamie.vicary/twovect/guide.pdf"} user guide
          %h2#whats_it_for What’s it for?
          %p
            The mathematics of 2-vector spaces is often referred to as
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "higher+algebra"} higher linear algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+algebra"} linear algebra
            required for calculations involving traditional vector spaces. This higher linear algebra can be difficult to work with by hand: whereas ordinary linear algebra involves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrices"} matrices
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            %em matrices
            of complex numbers. And whereas ordinary matrices can be composed in two different ways, ordinary composition and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horizontal+composition"} horizontal composition
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+composition"} vertical composition
          %p
            While the underlying mathematics of 2-vector spaces is elegant and natural, the combinatorics of these basic operations can make calculations difficult to perform by hand.
            %em TwoVect
            implements the basic operations of higher linear algebra, and can make calculations a lot easier.
          %p Here are some example uses for the package.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                You’ve worked out the associator and unitors for a
                %a{:href => "semisimple+category"} semisimple
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            %li
              %p
                You’ve worked out other structure, like a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "braided+monoidal+category"} braiding
                %a{:href => "symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetry
                or
                %a{:href => "ribbon+category"} ribbon
                structure, and you want to check the defining equations are satisfied.
            %li
              %p You want to use Mathematica’s built-in solvers to help find these structures.
            %li
              %p
                You want to calculate the value of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
                in a semisimple monoidal category.
            %li
              %p
                You want to check algebraic properties of a semisimple monoidal category. Does it have
                = succeed "?" do
                  %a{:href => "dualizable+object"} duals for objects
                = succeed "?" do
                  %a{:href => "modular+tensor+category"} modular
            %li
              %p
                You want to check the axioms are satisfied for an
                %a{:href => "monoid#inamonoidalcategory"} internal monoid object
                in a semisimple monoidal category, or you want Mathematica to help you find such an object.
            %li
              %p
                You have chosen higher linear maps to associate to the generators of some
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "higher+algebra"} higher algebraic structure
            %li
              %p
                You want to verify correctness of a quantum protocol, described in terms of the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1207.4563"} 2-categorical approach to quantum information
          %h2#how_does_it_work How does it work?
          %p
            The basic package
            %em TwoVect
            implements a completely skeletal version of
            = succeed "," do
              %strong 2Vect
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong 2-vector spaces
                are represented by natural numbers.
            %li
              %p
                %strong
                  %a{:href => "linear+functor"} Linear functors
                between 2-vector spaces are represented by matrices of natural numbers.
            %li
              %p
                %strong
                  %a{:href => "natural+transformation"} Natural transformations
                between linear functors are represented by matrices of matrices of complex numbers.
          %p
            The three primitive composition operations of
            %strong 2Vect
            are implemented:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong Tensor product
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊙
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot
                is a binary operation for which the arguments can be 0-cells, 1-cells or 2-cells.
            %li
              %p
                %strong Horizontal composition
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∘
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ
                is a binary operation, for which the arguments must 1-cells or 2-cells.
            %li
              %p
                %strong Vertical composition
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⋅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot
                is a binary operation for which the arguments must be 2-cells.
          %p The nonidentity invertible structural 2-cells are also implemented, which account for weakness of horizontal composition and tensor product:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed ":" do
                  %strong interchanger
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi τ
                        %mrow
                          %mi f
                          %mo ,
                          %mi g
                          %mo ,
                          %mi h
                          %mo ,
                          %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊙
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi h
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ⊙
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∘
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo ⊙
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau_{f,g,h,i} : (f \circ g) \odot (h \circ i) \to (f \odot h) \circ (g \odot i)
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed ":" do
                  %strong compositer
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ω
                        %mrow
                          %mi f
                          %mo ,
                          %mi g
                          %mo ,
                          %mi h
                      %mo :
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∘
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi h
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_{f,g,h} : f \circ (g \circ h) \to (f \circ g) \circ h
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed ":" do
                  %strong symmetrizer
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mrow
                          %mi f
                          %mo ,
                          %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} swap
                      %mo ∘
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ⊙
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo ⊙
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} swap
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_{f,g}: \mathrm{swap} \circ (f \odot g) \to (g \odot f) \circ \mathrm{swap}
          %p There are also two add-on packages:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em MTCategories
                provides implementations of the equations for symmetric or braided semisimple monoidal categories.
            %li
              %p
                %em MTCategory
                provides a simplified syntax for working ‘inside’ a particular choice of monoidal category. Once this is defined, the value of string diagrams can be computed by typing them in using ordinary string diagram syntax.
          %h2#how_could_this_be_developed How could this be developed?
          %p There are several exciting ways this could evolve. If you want to help out, get in touch! Lots of things on this list would involve original research, and could form a part of a Masters or PhD dissertation.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A graphical front end would be a powerful addition. Compare to
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://sites.google.com/site/quantomatic/"} Quantomatic
                %em ordinary
                linear algebra.
            %li
              %p
                Can
                %em TwoVect
                be connected in a useful way to
                = succeed "?" do
                  %a{:href => "Rocq"} computational methods in higher type theory
            %li
              %p
                It should be possible to write code that inserts the structural isomorphisms
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi τ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                %em automatically
                where necessary. This would make the package enormously more powerful for certain applications.
            %li
              %p Many more things could be implemented - a test for modularity of a braided monoidal category, quantum doubles, Hopf algebra axioms, module category axioms …
          %p There are also some more mundane issues that need dealing with.
          %ul
            %li
              %p The basic functions that perform 2-cell tensor product, horizontal composition and vertical composition haven’t been optimized, and run slowly for large inputs. There should be a lot of scope for improving the code, and perhaps also for compilation, which Mathematica supports.
            %li
              %p There are some other strange issues around speed; different ways to tell Mathematica to compute the same thing can give rise to very different calculation times.
            %li
              %p We’re not sure how to test systematically whether everything has been implemented correctly.
            %li
              %p There is probably some scope to restructure the packages and make them easier to use.
          .property
